# Line endings.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows but has to work on macOS, Linux, and now
# inside a Linux container — which is where CRLF causes failures that look like
# anything but a line-ending problem:
#
#   * a shell script with CRLF fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M"
#   * a .dockerignore with CRLF leaves a stray \r on every pattern, so `storage/`
#     stops matching and the team's live database gets baked into the image
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` (set on this machine) hands CRLF back on checkout, so the
# files that must not have it are pinned explicitly. `eol=lf` wins over autocrlf.

# Normalize everything to LF inside the repository; check out native.
* text=auto

# Anything a Unix shell or Docker executes or parses must keep LF in the working
# tree too, on every platform.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# PowerShell, batch and the Task Scheduler XML stay CRLF, which is what Windows
# tooling expects.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.xml text eol=crlf

# A generated lockfile: collapse it in diffs and keep it out of language stats.
frontend/package-lock.json linguist-generated=true -diff

# Never touch binaries.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.step binary
*.stp binary
*.stl binary
*.sldprt binary
*.sldasm binary
